				<div class="info">
					<span class="printuser avatarhover"><a href="http://www.wikidot.com/user:info/rounderhouse" onclick="WIKIDOT.page.listeners.userInfo(4187885); return false;"  ><img class="small" src="http://www.wikidot.com/avatar.php?userid=4187885&amp;amp;size=small&amp;amp;timestamp=1605795097" alt="Rounderhouse" style="background-image:url(http://www.wikidot.com/userkarma.php?u=4187885)"/></a><a href="http://www.wikidot.com/user:info/rounderhouse" onclick="WIKIDOT.page.listeners.userInfo(4187885); return false;" >Rounderhouse</a></span> <span class="odate time_1588372034 format_%25e%20%25b%20%25Y%2C%20%25H%3A%25M%7Cagohover">01 May 2020 22:27</span>
														</div>
			</div>
			<div class="content" id="post-content-4624695">
									

<p>This is trying to beat me over the head with pathos. The first rule of writing emotional feel-good articles is that you shouldn't write an article with the intent of being feel-good, you should try to tell a story and let the emotions come out naturally through character interaction. That's not whats happening here. Even from the interview, you hammer in the details that this is a poor village and priest who uses the anomaly to feed his people, altruistic, etc. The Conproc revisions are super heavy-handed in their elaboration of it.</p>
<blockquote>
<p>Given the fact that he lost his means to do that, as well as the fabricated drug bust against him, his life is in a significantly lower spot than before. Giving SCP-5246 back to him will restore his life, and the people’s faith in him.</p>
<hr />
<p>I know that the Foundation can be cold, but taking a priest's method to feed the ones around him is cruel. We're not cruel, right?</p>
</blockquote>
<p>Like, you could've just put a log of the suggested revision without the heartfelt explanation why, that just ruins any emotional weight it had. This ties back to the first rule; <em>appearing</em> like you're trying to pull the reader's heartstrings will kill any chance you have of doing so, because then they're aware of it.<br />
I don't really have much to say on the front of the interview, but I have to note that the article also has a big ol' case of note-at-the-end syndrome. Not in that the note explains whats going on, but it's&#8230;. it's like a TV episode ending and then one of the characters turns to the camera and goes &quot;And the moral of the story is&#8230;.&quot;. It's just corny, and undercuts the effect that the reader should naturally come to without having one of the characters explain it to them directly.</p>

							</div>

								
					
